 "footnotes": [
  {
   "id": "c-majorana",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "The book prints “Majorana flops”. These spin flips are known as Majorana flips."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-quadrupole-sum",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "The book prints $\\sum|\\tilde{B}_i| = 0$, which with the absolute values holds only for a vanishing field. Maxwell's equation $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{B} = 0$ requires $\\sum_i\\tilde{B}_i = 0$: the gradients along the three axes cancel, as the anti-Helmholtz field's $B'$, $B'$ and $-2B'$ do."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-fig-11-4-cited",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "The book cites Figure 11.4. The lowered trap depth is drawn in Figure 11.3; Figure 11.4 is the RF knife of the next paragraph."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-eq-11-29",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "The thermalization rate of an atom goes as its own collision rate, $\\kGammath \\propto \\knzero\\kvbar\\ksigmael$; $\\knzero^2\\kvbar\\ksigmael$ is the number of collisions per unit volume and time. And $\\ksigmael$ is the cross-section for elastic collisions, not their rate."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-table-11-3-electric",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "An electric field traps a ground-state atom through its polarizability, with a depth of order $\\frac{1}{2}\\alpha E^2$. For sodium ($\\alpha \\approx 24$ Å³) that is about 1 µK at 1 kV/cm and 10 mK at 100 kV/cm, the depths the table lists; fields of 1–100 MV/cm would give depths a million times larger. The laboratory field strengths are 1–100 kV/cm."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-table-11-3-gravity",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "Gravity holds an atom of mass $m$ below a height $h$ with a depth $mgh$, and $mg/k_B$ is 1.2 mK per metre for hydrogen, 27 mK/m for sodium and 0.10 K/m for ⁸⁷Rb, above the range printed for atoms and molecules alike."
  }
 ],
