  {
   "id": "linear-trap-atom-number",
   "what": "the number of atoms in a linear trap",
   "source": "Modern Atomic and Optical Physics II, 11.3 Experimental techniques used in Bose condensation of atoms, 11.3.6",
   "nodes": [
    {
     "assumes": [
      {
       "tex": "\\kndens(\\rho) = \\knzero e^{-\\kmumag|B(\\rho)|/k_B\\kT}",
       "src": "(11.17)"
      },
      {
       "tex": "B(\\rho) = \\frac{B_0}{\\rho_0}\\rho",
       "src": "(11.16)"
      },
      {
       "text": "The density of atoms near the edge of the trap is negligibly small when $\\ketaev > 5$, so the upper limit $\\rho_0$ of the integral can be set to $\\infty$.",
       "src": "11.3.6"
      }
     ],
     "then": {
      "tex": "\\mk{N}{N} = \\mk{I}{\\int d^3\\mathbf{r}\\,\\kndens(\\rho)}",
      "uses": []
     },
     "steps": [
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{N}{N} = \\mk{I}{\\int d^3\\mathbf{r}\\,\\knzero e^{-\\kmumag|B(\\rho)|/k_B\\kT}}",
       "uses": [
        0
       ]
      },
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{N}{N} = \\mk{I}{\\int_0^{\\rho_0} d\\rho\\, 4\\pi\\rho^2\\knzero e^{-\\overbrace{(\\kmumag B_0/k_B\\kT)}^{\\ketaev}(\\rho/\\rho_0)}}",
       "uses": [
        1
       ],
       "cap": "The density depends on $\\rho$ alone, so $d^3\\mathbf{r} = 4\\pi\\rho^2 d\\rho$, out to the trap size $\\rho_0$."
      },
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{N}{N} \\approx \\mk{I}{\\frac{4\\pi\\rho_0^3}{\\ketaev^3}\\knzero\\int_0^\\infty du\\, u^2e^{-u}}",
       "uses": [
        2
       ],
       "cap": "Substituting $u = \\eta\\rho/\\rho_0$, so that $\\rho^2 d\\rho = (\\rho_0/\\eta)^3 u^2 du$."
      },
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{N}{N} \\approx \\mk{I}{6\\underbrace{\\frac{4\\pi\\rho_0^3}{3}}_{\\kVgeo}\\frac{\\knzero}{\\ketaev^3}}",
       "uses": [],
       "cap": "$\\int_0^\\infty u^2 e^{-u}du = 2$, and $2 \\cdot 4\\pi\\rho_0^3 = 6 \\cdot \\frac{4\\pi\\rho_0^3}{3}$."
      }
     ],
     "num": "(11.21)",
     "note": "The book prints (11.21) without $\\knzero$: $6\\kVgeo/\\ketaev^3$. The integral (11.20) carries $\\knzero$, so $N = 6\\kVgeo\\knzero/\\ketaev^3$."
    }
   ]
